<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sun Dance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sun Dance is a major religious ceremony of many Plains Indigenous peoples, including the Lakota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a>, Arapaho and Blackfoot. Held in summer around a central pole, it involves fasting, prayer, dancing and, among some nations, ritual piercing, as an offering for the renewal of the people and the world. The US and Canadian governments banned or restricted it from the 1880s, but it continued in secret and is again widely practised.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-dance.jpg" alt="Sun Dance" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Hall_Reservation._Shoshone_Indian_Sun_Dance_-_NARA_-_298649.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Sun Dance is a ceremony of some Native Americans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and of Indigenous peoples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, primarily of the Plains. Members of otherwise independent bands gather to reaffirm beliefs about the world and the supernatural through rituals of personal and community sacrifice. Common features are dances and songs passed down through generations, a traditional drum, a sacred fire, prayer with a ceremonial pipe and fasting from food and water before the dance. Communities plan for at least a year, one leader or a small group is in charge, and many elders advise. Family and friends stay in the surrounding camp and pray for the dancers.</p><p>In many ceremonies, particularly among the Lakota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> and Arapaho, dancers are pierced through the skin of the chest or back with skewers attached by thongs to the central pole or to heavy objects such as a bison skull, and they pull until the skin tears free. Not all nations pierce. The Plains Cree Thirst Dance involves fasting from water and strenuous movement around the pole, and among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshoni</a> the dance centres on prolonged fasting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Practitioners and scholars describe the dance as an offering made for the people rather than as punishment. The Oglala Lakota holy man Black Elk told Joseph Epes Brown that the dancer offers his body and soul for the sake of the people. The dance takes place in a circular lodge around a cottonwood pole, which represents the axis mundi, the centre of the world, and its 28 posts are often read as lunar cycles or cosmic balance. Ruth Underhill called it a rite of renewal, and Ella Cara Deloria, a Dakota scholar, wrote that it signified an offering of one's own body in thanks or petition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> both passed laws meant to suppress Indigenous cultures and force assimilation, and the Sun Dance was among the banned ceremonies. In Canada, Indian agents interfered with it from 1882 until the 1940s, and an 1895 amendment to the Indian Act outlawed any dance involving wounding of the body. The Plains Cree, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saulteaux">Saulteaux</a> and Blackfoot kept holding it, some in secret, some with the agents' permission and some without piercing. Canada dropped the ban on flesh-wounding in 1951, and in 1978 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> passed the American Indian Religious Freedom Act.</p><p>The Sun Dance is a living tradition practised each year in many Native communities. Filming a ceremony is forbidden in most Sun Dance cultures. In 1993 the Lakota Summit V, about 500 representatives of 40 peoples and bands, condemned what it called exploitation of Lakota spirituality, and in 2003 the Keeper of the Sacred White Buffalo Calf Pipe asked non-Native people not to approach the altars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sun Dance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mounted bison hunting supplied food, hides and trade goods, and ceremonies such as the <b>Sun Dance</b> renewed communal bonds.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
